# Language navigation

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Language navigation links to the same content in another language, such as a help page in English and Welsh. Use it on your own pages when a translated version of the current content exists.

Home Assistant has no language navigation element. Each person picks the interface language in the language setting of their Home Assistant profile. So the pattern is for your own HTML pages. To offer a dashboard in another language, link to the other dashboard with a plain link (see [Link / anchor](link-anchor.html)).

This page covers:

- Language navigation
  - Civica Language Navigation Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- A navigation area named “Language”.
- A list of the languages, one item for each.
- The current language, marked and not a link.
- A link for each other language, with its language set.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-language-navigation-pattern"><code>Civica Language Navigation Pattern</code></h3>

This is markup for your own pages. Civica has no card or element for it.

```html
<nav aria-label="Language">
  <ul>
    <li><span aria-current="true" lang="en">English</span></li>
    <li><a href="/cy/heating.html" rel="alternate" lang="cy" hreflang="cy">Cymraeg</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>
```

- Write each language name in that language: “Cymraeg”, not “Welsh”.
- Mark the current language with `aria-current="true"` and do not make it a link.
- Put `lang` and `hreflang` on every link to another language.
- Set `lang` on the page to match its main content. A passage in another language needs its own `lang`.
- Put the navigation in the same place on every page.

**What the Civica theme sets.** Nothing in the Home Assistant theme styles this, because Home Assistant has no language navigation. Links in a Markdown card take `--primary-color` (see [Link / anchor](link-anchor.html)).

Only publish a translation after a suitably qualified speaker has reviewed it. Say when a translation is missing or out of date. Do not replace a reviewed household instruction with machine translation.

British Sign Language needs its own content. A language menu is not a substitute: see [Help people find British Sign Language content](../patterns/british-sign-language.html).

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It keeps people on the same topic in their own language.
- It needs a reviewed translation for every page you list.
- It is plain links, so it works without script.
- It does not change the Home Assistant interface language, entity names or translations.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Take the person to the same topic, not an unrelated home page. Never choose a language from a person’s name or from where they are. Do not use flags, because a flag stands for a country and not a language. Say clearly when no translation exists. Never change a language when someone is part-way through a form and would lose their work.

Test the real destinations, the page title, the browser Back button, long translated names and right-to-left layouts where they apply. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

Success criteria that apply:

- 3.1.1 Language of Page and 3.1.2 Language of Parts: `lang` on the page and on every link and passage in another language.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context): each link names a language.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: the current language is marked.
- 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation: the same place on every page.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: long labels must wrap.

One list, in the same place every time, keeps the effort low.

